    "budgetUnknown": true,
    "sourceUrl": "https://techspecs.ui.com/uisp/wired/es-48-750w",
    "note": "NO PUBLISHED TOTAL POE BUDGET. The page prints, under Hardware, \"Power supply: AC/DC, internal, 750W\" and \"Max. power consumption: Includes PoE output: 750W; Excludes PoE output: 64W\"; under PoE Per Port it prints only \"Max. PoE+ wattage per port by PSE: 34.2W\" and \"Max. passive PoE wattage per port: 17W\". Unlike its 500 W sibling there is no \"Total PoE available\" row, so 750 W is the PSU/whole-box ceiling - the arithmetic ceiling for PDs would be 750-64 = 686 W, and that subtraction is ours, not Ubiquiti's. Modelled unknown on purpose, the same call made for ES-24-500W: a PSU rating must not be served as a PSE allocation. The 48 ports and their 802.3at class are modelled, so the engine reports the ports and refuses the arithmetic instead of powering a lab from a derived number."
  },
  "notes": "Census from the vendor tech-spec table (https://techspecs.ui.com/uisp/wired/es-48-750w, HTTP 200, 582,768 B, server-rendered table, read 2026-11-20): Networking interface \"(48) 10/100/1000 Mbps RJ45 Ethernet ports; (2) 1/10 Gbps SFP+ Ethernet ports; (2) 1 Gbps SFP Ethernet ports\"; PoE Per Port \"PoE/PoE+ (Pins 1, 2+; 3, 6-); 24V DC passive PoE (Pins 4, 5+; 7, 8-)\"; Management interface \"(1) RJ45 serial port, Ethernet In/Out-Band\". Before this pass the 48 copper ports carried no connector, no poeOut and no budget at all. Also added: the serial console (52 jacks for 53 physical), the connector on the two 1G SFP cages, and the 10M rung, which this page states explicitly as \"10/100/1000 Mbps\" (the 500 W sibling prints only \"GbE\", hence the difference between the two files)."
}
